#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tHOW TO ADD CUSTOM SCRIPTS TO THE JHALFS MAKEFILE\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:9
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Normally JHALFS creates a Makefile containing only those scripts found in\n"
"the {,B}LFS books. An automated construction tool cannot predict the\n"
"needs of every individual and requests are made \"Can you add xxxx package\".\n"
"Rather than adding numerous package scripts and switches for each request it\n"
"was easier to add a tool for the user(s) to code their own package needs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:13
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  There are two areas that can be customized: how the base system is built\n"
"and what additional configurations and packages your hardware requires to be\n"
"able to boot and run. Each of those areas are handled in a different way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "        BASE SYSTEM CUSTOMIZATION\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "  There are two ways to alter how the base system will be built:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#: README.CUSTOM:23
msgid ""
"Using a working copy of the book sources and editing the XML files.  This is "
"the way used by book editors to test packages upgrades, command changes, "
"build order changes. etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:26
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    This method requires you know very well the book sources and what\n"
"    files need be edited. It will not be discussed here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#: README.CUSTOM:29
msgid ""
"Editing the generated build scripts to make any change you want.  This is "
"the method discussed below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:32
#, no-wrap
msgid "  EDITING THE BASE SCRIPTS\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:36
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  To begin with, the build scripts should be generated with book defaults. To\n"
"do that, configure jhalfs activating any option you want included, but do not\n"
"select \"Run the Makefile\" option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:40
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Under the ${BUILD_DIR}/${SCRIPT_ROOT}/$COMMANDS directory\n"
"(using the defaults values to do an LFS build, that directory name is\n"
"/mnt/build_dir/jhalfs/lfs-commands) you will find the default build scripts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:46
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  If all you want is modify, add, or remove some command from a package\n"
"installation, for example to change its ./configure line, just edit the related\n"
"script. If changing or adding a patch, be sure to copy the new patch to the\n"
"${BUILD_DIR}/sources directory. When done, run 'make' from inside the\n"
"${BUILD_DIR}/${SCRIPT_ROOT} directory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "  REPLACING OR INSERTING PACKAGES AND CHANGING BUILD ORDER\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "  To remove a package from the system, just remove its script(s).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:56
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  To change the version of some package, or to build a newer or older version\n"
"than that in the book, edit ${BUILD_DIR}/${SCRIPT_ROOT}/pkg_tarball_list to\n"
"change its tarball name and place the new tarball in the ${BUILD_DIR}/sources\n"
"directory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  To replace a package by an equivalent one, rename the replaced package script\n"
"to reflect the new package name (for example, 102-man-db -> 102-man), edit the\n"
"script to make the required commands changes, place the new tarball in the\n"
"${BUILD_DIR}/sources directory, and edit ${BUILD_DIR}/${SCRIPT_ROOT}/pkg_tarball_list\n"
"file to replace the removed package tarball name by the new package tarball\n"
"name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:66
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  To change the build order, rename the scripts changing the first 3-digits\n"
"string until they are sorted in the way you want.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  To insert a new package, for example to build Cracklib in order to build\n"
"Shadow with Cracklib support, you should first decide before what package it\n"
"needs to be installed, in this example 107-shadow. Then create a new script\n"
"containing the needed commands, using an existing one as template, and name it\n"
"with the same 3-digits string used for that mentioned default package, but\n"
"adding another 1-digit string. In our example, the new script to build Cracklib\n"
"before Shadow will be named 107-1-cracklib. This naming scheme allows inserting\n"
"up to 10 scripts before each of the existing scripts. Place the tarball for\n"
"the new package and required patches, if any, in ${BUILD_DIR}/sources and edit\n"
"${BUILD_DIR}/${SCRIPT_ROOT}/pkg_tarball_list to add the tarball name for that\n"
"package.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  When ready, launch again the jhalfs configuration interface. Make sure that\n"
"exactly the same options are selected as when generating the default build\n"
"scripts. Be sure that \"Rebuild files\" is unselected and select \"Run the\n"
"Makefile\" if you want. Then select \"Rebuild the Makefile\". This will create a\n"
"new Makefile based on the changes you made to the build scripts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "       ADDING POST-SYSTEM BUILD CONFIGURATION FILES AND EXTRA PACKAGES\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The installation of BLFS packages is handled via blfs-tool and activated\n"
"when you select the appropriate menu option. See README and README.BLFS for\n"
"more info.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:97
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The feature described below was added so users could install remaining\n"
"configuration files, build the packages necessary to access the Internet\n"
"or to support specific hardware, or to install basic utilities that are\n"
"needed from the beginning, and was not intended to replace the BLFS\n"
"install system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  LAYOUT\n"
"  A new directory has been added to JHALFS tree which contains the\n"
"configuration scripts and a few examples. A switch has been added to the\n"
"configuration file which enables/disables the inclusion of custom scripts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    custom\n"
"      /config          <-- where to put your scripts.\n"
"      /examples        <-- a few example scripts\n"
"      template         <-- ALL scripts MUST look like this\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:111
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  NOTE::: You are responsible for including all dependencies and ensuring they\n"
"          are built in the right order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  1. To add a package to the final JHALFS Makefile you must first create a file\n"
"     in the custom/config directory. The directory custom/config may need\n"
"     to be created first.\n"
"       **All config files MUST follow the naming convention, xxx-PKG, where xxx\n"
"       is the order number and PKG is the name of the package. The file naming\n"
"       format is important as it defines the build order. The example shown\n"
"       below has an order number 950 and log files will list in alphabetical\n"
"       order in the /logs directory after blfs-tools scripts.\n"
"    The simplest method is to copy the template file into the config directory\n"
"    and rename it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  2. Populate the variables with the necessary values.\n"
"       Variable function is self explanatory. PATCH variables must be\n"
"       numbered, starting at PATCH1 (up to PATCH10). If the md5 checksum value\n"
"       is known, it can be added after the URL as in (quotes needed):\n"
"       PATCH1=\"http://patch-host/patch-path xxxxxxx...\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:136
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  3. Build commands\n"
"       If the package you want to include is found in the BLFS\n"
"       book then you only need to copy/paste the commands between the xEOFx\n"
"       text, otherwise you will need to define the commands yourself.\n"
"     NOTE::: This script you just created is not usable directly but contains\n"
"             all the information necessary for jhalfs to create a build script\n"
"\t     and an entry in the jhalfs Makefile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  4. As mentioned previously the build order is dictated by the 3 digit number\n"
"     in the file name. If a package has dependencies it must be numerically\n"
"     larger than the dependency files.\n"
"       e.g. The package mc has glib as a dependency, so the build order is:\n"
"           950-glib\n"
"           951-mc\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  5. '
#: README.CUSTOM:148
msgid ""
"A config file for BLFS-bootscripts is already created as 999-"
"blfs_bootscripts.  If a package requires a bootscript to be installed add "
"the cmd to this file and NOT in the package script. The gpm script is "
"included as an example of patch file and the need for a blfs bootscript."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "  EXAMPLES\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Other examples can be found in custom/examples. They are dated and\n"
"    unmaintained, but can be useful for creating your own scripts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:157
msgid "#--------- GLIB example -----------"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:163
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  #\n"
"  # Filename syntax xxx-PKG  ie. 950-glibc\n"
"  # Create a file in the custom/config directory\n"
"  # Populate the file using the following script as an example\n"
"  #\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:169
msgid ""
"PKG=\"glib\" PKG_VERSION=\"1.2.10\" PKG_FILE=\"glib-1.2.10.tar.gz\" "
"URL=\"http://gd.tuwien.ac.at/graphics/gimp/gtk/v1.2/${PKG_FILE}\" "
"MD5=\"6fe30dad87c77b91b632def29dd69ef9\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  # The following code removes previously defined PATCHx variables\n"
"for i in PATCH{1..10}; do\n"
"   unset $i\n"
"done\n"
"  # Patches are named PATCH[1..10]\n"
"  # This information is used to download the patch only\n"
"PATCH1=\"http://www.linuxfromscratch.org/patches/blfs/svn/glib-1.2.10-gcc34-1.patch 0077a1cce5e8a2231ac5a9b08c6263ba\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  # NOTE::\n"
"  #  The convoluted scheme used to write out a temporary file is\n"
"  #  a work-around for embedded single and double quotes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:184 README.CUSTOM:211 README.CUSTOM:258
msgid "( cat << \"xEOFx\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:190
msgid ""
"patch -Np1 -i ../glib-1.2.10-gcc34-1.patch && ./configure --prefix=/usr && "
"make make install && chmod -v 755 /usr/lib/libgmodule-1.2.so.0.0.10"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:193 README.CUSTOM:237 README.CUSTOM:262
msgid "xEOFx ) > tmp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:196
msgid "#--------- GPM example -----------"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:208
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"PKG=\"gpm\"\n"
"PKG_VERSION=\"1.20.1\"\n"
"PKG_FILE=\"gpm-1.20.1.tar.bz2\"\n"
"URL=\"ftp://arcana.linux.it/pub/gpm/gpm-1.20.1.tar.bz2\"\n"
"MD5=\"2c63e827d755527950d9d13fe3d87692\"\n"
"for i in PATCH{1..10}; do\n"
"   unset $i\n"
"done\n"
"PATCH1=\" http://www.linuxfromscratch.org/patches/blfs/svn/gpm-1.20.1-segfault-1.patch\"\n"
"PATCH2=\" http://www.linuxfromscratch.org/patches/blfs/svn/gpm-1.20.1-silent-1.patch\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:216
msgid ""
"patch -Np1 -i ../gpm-1.20.1-segfault-1.patch && patch -Np1 -i ../gpm-1.20.1-"
"silent-1.patch && ./configure --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc && LDFLAGS=\"-"
"lm\" make"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:220
msgid "make install && cp -v conf/gpm-root.conf /etc && ldconfig"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:224
msgid ""
"# The normal cmd to install the boot script for gpm # --- PUT THIS CMD "
"INSIDE 999-blfs_bootscripts #make install-gpm"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:227
msgid "cat > /etc/sysconfig/mouse << \"EOF\" # Begin /etc/sysconfig/mouse"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:231
msgid "MDEVICE=\"/dev/psaux\" PROTOCOL=\"imps2\" GPMOPTS=\"\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:234
msgid "# End /etc/sysconfig/mouse EOF"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#--------- CMDS ONLY example -----------\n"
"  # This is an example of a self contained cmd script\n"
"  # There are no referenced to a package or package dir.\n"
"  # This method is useful for creating user files/profiles/etc\n"
"  # at build time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:255
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"PKG=\"\"\n"
"PKG_VERSION=\"\"\n"
"PKG_FILE=\"\"\n"
"URL=\"\"\n"
"MD5=\"\"\n"
"for i in PATCH{1..10}; do\n"
"   unset $i\n"
"done\n"
"PATCH1=\"\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README.CUSTOM:260
msgid "echo \"JUST A USELESS TRACE\""
msgstr ""
